-- title:
--   Theorem 3.A.4 — Strassen's martingale-coupling characterization of the convex order
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ on $(\Omega,\mu)$ and $Y$ on $(\Omega',\nu)$ be random variables. Then
--
--   $$X \le_{cx} Y \iff \exists\,(\Omega'',\rho),\ \hat X,\hat Y:\Omega''\to\mathbb{R} \text{ with }
--     \hat X =_{st} X,\ \hat Y =_{st} Y,\ \text{and } \{\hat X,\hat Y\} \text{ a martingale},$$
--
--   i.e. $E[\hat Y \mid \hat X] = \hat X$ a.s. **Furthermore**, $\hat X$ and $\hat Y$ can be
--   selected such that $[\hat Y \mid \hat X = x]$ is increasing in $x$ in the usual stochastic
--   order $\le_{st}$ — the conditional law of $\hat Y$ given $\hat X = x$ stochastically increases
--   with $x$. This is Strassen's martingale-coupling characterization of the convex order (the
--   mean-preserving spread of Rothschild–Stiglitz outside OR/economics circles): $X \le_{cx} Y$
--   holds exactly when a copy of each can be built on a common probability space so that the copy
--   of $Y$ is, conditionally on the copy of $X$, a fair (martingale) randomization of it whose
--   conditional law only grows (in $\le_{st}$) as the conditioning value grows. It is the archetype
--   every other chapter's own coupling theorem in this series restates for its own order, and the
--   book itself calls the constructive direction "not easy to prove" — no proof is attempted here.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "$\hat X =_{st} X$" is `ProbabilityTheory.IdentDistrib`, as in Chapter
--   I's Theorem 1.A.1. "$E[\hat Y\mid\hat X]=\hat X$ a.s." is Mathlib's own conditional-expectation
--   notation `ρ[Ŷ | m] =ᵐ[ρ] X̂` with `m` the σ-algebra generated by `X̂`
--   (`MeasurableSpace.comap X̂ inferInstance`), exactly the martingale API's convention, not an
--   informal gloss. The "Furthermore" clause is folded into the same existential witness (the book
--   states it as a strengthening of the same construction, not a separate claim) using Mathlib's
--   `ProbabilityTheory.condDistrib`, the regular conditional distribution kernel of `Ŷ` given `X̂`:
--   `[Ŷ | X̂ = x]` is `condDistrib Ŷ X̂ ρ x`, and "increasing in `x` in `≤st`" is stated as the
--   kernel's survival function `t ↦ (condDistrib Ŷ X̂ ρ x) {y | t < y}` being monotone in `x` at
--   every threshold `t` — the same tail-probability shape `UsualOrder` uses in Chapter I, restated
--   locally since drafts cannot import another mission's definitions. Both directions of the `↔`
--   remain true with this strengthened witness: the reverse direction (existence ⟹ `X ≤cx Y`) only
--   uses the martingale conjunct (Jensen's inequality, per the book's own proof sketch), so the
--   extra monotonicity conjunct does not change what the converse needs.
-- source:
--   Shaked & Shanthikumar, Stochastic Orders, Springer 2007, p. 112, Theorem 3.A.4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Convex_ConvexOrder

namespace StochasticOrders.Convex

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- Theorem 3.A.4 (Shaked & Shanthikumar, *Stochastic Orders*, Springer 2007, p. 112): `X ≤cx Y`
if, and only if, there exist two random variables `X̂` and `Ŷ`, defined on the same probability
space, such that `X̂ =st X`, `Ŷ =st Y`, and `{X̂, Ŷ}` is a martingale, that is,
`E[Ŷ | X̂] = X̂` a.s. Furthermore (the theorem's second sentence, folded into the same witness
here since it strengthens the same existential claim), `X̂` and `Ŷ` can be selected such that
`[Ŷ | X̂ = x]` is increasing in `x` in the usual stochastic order `≤st`: the conditional
distribution of `Ŷ` given `X̂ = x`, as a kernel from `ℝ` to `ℝ`, has an increasing survival
function in `x` at every threshold `t`. -/
theorem convex_order_martingale_coupling_iff {Ω Ω' : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    [MeasurableSpace Ω'] (μ : Measure Ω) (ν : Measure Ω') [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    [IsProbabilityMeasure ν] (X : Ω → ℝ) (Y : Ω' → ℝ) :
    ConvexOrder μ ν X Y ↔
      ∃ (Ω'' : Type) (_ : MeasurableSpace Ω'') (ρ : Measure Ω'') (_ : IsProbabilityMeasure ρ)
        (Xhat Yhat : Ω'' → ℝ),
        IdentDistrib Xhat X ρ μ ∧ IdentDistrib Yhat Y ρ ν ∧
        ρ[Yhat | MeasurableSpace.comap Xhat inferInstance] =ᵐ[ρ] Xhat ∧
        (∀ x₁ x₂ : ℝ, x₁ ≤ x₂ → ∀ t : ℝ,
          (condDistrib Yhat Xhat ρ x₁) {y : ℝ | t < y} ≤
            (condDistrib Yhat Xhat ρ x₂) {y : ℝ | t < y}) := by sorry

end StochasticOrders.Convex
